      story: [{"t": "baaskaraa is an Indian artist living in Singapore: a painter, sculptor, poet and digital artist.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}, {"t": "He was born Baskar in 1974 in Irumbulikkurichi, a village in Tamil Nadu, India. He won first prizes in painting at school three years running, then trained at the Government College of Arts and Crafts, Chennai, India's oldest art school, founded in 1850, graduating with a Bachelor of Fine Arts, First Class with Distinction.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}, {"t": "While still a student, his work was shown at the 40th National Exhibition of Art in Bangalore and acquired by the National Gallery of Modern Art. In 1998 he received the Victoria Technical Institute's Mahatma Gandhi Birth Centenary Memorial Award for My Native Land, and the Tamil Nadu state award for Light on Scape Series-1. In May 2000 he held his first solo exhibition, at Apparao Galleries, Chennai, sponsored by the Tamil Nadu state art body.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}, {"t": "The painter who fell into language", "fs": "22px", "fw": "600", "c": "#1A1A1D", "mt": "14px"}, {"t": "In 2000 he landed in Singapore with a painter's hands and no canvas. What he found instead was a language: his own.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}, {"t": "For nearly a decade he worked inside Tamil, building it in pixels and sound for the children of a new country. He drew it, animated it and edited its voice, frame by frame and syllable by syllable. Day after day the language passed through his hands, and something turned. His thoughts stopped arriving as images. They arrived as words.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}, {"t": "So he wrote.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}, {"t": "In 2003 he married Suguna, a poet. Two writers at one table, then twice a month at Kavimaalai and Kavi Solai, the Tamil poetry circles where Singapore's poets read and are judged by each other. It went on for over a decade. The circles became the national stage: the Golden Point Award for Tamil Poetry in 2011, and again in 2013, when Suguna took first prize in the same ceremony. Two poets, one home, one language.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}, {"t": "Then the words came back to the canvas. Today a work by baaskaraa begins as a thought, becomes a line, and only then becomes a form, often a whole series. Bird Sanctum was the first time the public saw it: a Tamil novel turned into nests of real twigs, touring Singapore's national libraries in 2014.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}, {"t": "The work also left the studio. In 2013, for Maya Dance Theatre's production GREY, he and the photographer Charlie Lim made the murals on the rooftop of the Singai Tamil Sangam in Little India, guiding the dancers, residents and students who painted them. For four nights in June the walls were the stage. The murals have since been painted over; the photographs remain.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}, {"t": "He doesn't illustrate literature. He writes it, and then he builds it.", "fs": "17px", "fw": "400", "c": "#3A3A3A", "mt": "0"}],
